course: dictionary
lesson: 3850
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word foundation is a good example. Look at the words around it, because they point to the right meaning.
explain: «Builders dug deep foundations, so the tall tower would not sink into the ground.» The words builders, tower and sink show that foundation means the strong base under a building. Try your meaning in the sentence: "the strong base under a building, so the tower would not sink" still makes sense.
explain: «Reading is the foundation of learning.» There is no building here. The word learning shows a different meaning: the basic part on which other things are built. Swap in "basic part" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Foundation means the strong base under a building, usually under the ground, that holds it up.
fact: «Foundations spread a building's weight into the ground.»
fact: Foundation can also mean the basic idea or part on which something else is built, as in «a solid foundation» for learning.
fact: «Lay the foundations» means to start building something, or to set up the basic parts before the rest.
fact: Foundation can also mean a charity that gives money for good causes, or a skin-coloured make-up cream.
exercise: «Builders also dig deep foundations, so a tall tower does not sink into the ground or lean.» What are foundations in this sentence? | A kind of skin cream | A charity that gives money | *The strong base under the tower that holds it up | A type of cake
exercise: «Reading is the foundation of learning.» What does foundation mean here? | *The basic part on which other learning is built | The strong base under a school | The starting day of a holiday | A make-up cream
exercise: «A skyscraper is a strong steel or concrete core and frame standing on deep foundations; its glass walls are only a skin.» Find and copy the word that tells you the core is strong. | walls | glass | *strong | skin
test: «The charity foundation gave money to the school library.» What does foundation mean here? | *An organisation that gives money for good causes | A skin-coloured make-up cream | The first day of a new school year | A strong base under a building
test: «Good habits give children a solid foundation for life.» What does foundation mean here? | A sad or tired feeling | *A basic base that other things are built on | A cream to cover spots | A tall, strong building
exam: «Her foundation looked smooth, and nobody could see her spots.» What does foundation mean here? | *A skin-coloured make-up cream | The starting of a school | An organisation that gives money | A strong base under a house
